This file has instructions for building and installing IMOD on supported
architectures, with as much ancillary information as possible.

In general, IMOD is not easy to build except on versions of Linux with Qt 4
installed, and our focus is on providing binary packages that will run on a
reasonably wide range of systems rather than on making IMOD easier to build.
Our intention in releasing IMOD source is to support programming with IMOD,
not to replace the use of binary packages for installation of IMOD.  If you
are a system administrator who habitually builds packages, please try to use
the appropriate binary packages first.  Aside from saving the build step, the
packages are a lot easier to upgrade. Some brief instructions for building
IMOD on Debian and Ubuntu are provided at the end, based on a report from
Andrew Jewett.

GENERAL NOTES

IMOD will compile with Qt versions of 4.5 or higher, including Qt 5. We now
use Qt 5.9.3 or 5.12.4 for current Windows and Mac builds, 5.12.4 for RHEL7
Linux, 5.10.1 for older Mac builds, and 4.6 - 4.8 in builds for other older
systems. 

The take home message: if you have Qt 4.5 or higher on your system already,
you can safely build with that.  If you need to build or otherwise install Qt,
it is safe to use the latest Qt compatible with your compilers on any
architecture, including Qt 5. 

Several strategies were used when building Qt.  One was to build in a local
directory and then run "make install".  With later versions on Mac, this
became problematic, and the strategy there has been to build in a local
directory, with the "install directory" being a link in /usr/local/Trolltech
to the local directory.  In these cases, "make install" was not run.  Below we
show the commands that we have used for building Qt, but these are just
examples and not authoritative statements.  You will probably be better off
following Qt instructions or avoiding a build by installing pre-built
binaries, as we have done with Qt 5.4 on Mac.

In all architectures, in order for the IMOD setup to work, environment
variable QTDIR must be defined to be the directory containing at least Qt/bin,
$QTDIR/bin must be on the path, and environment variable QMAKESPEC must be
defined.

The setup scripts modify some or all of the Makefiles produced by qmake.
On Windows, this is essential to make them work with cygwin make; elsewhere it
is needed for getting ctfplotter to link correctly and to adjust some compiler
settings.  This means that if you change a Qt project file, you should always
regenerate the Makefile by running setup rather than by running qmake in the
program directory.

In order to build with CUDA support, environment variable CUDA_DIR must be
defined to point to the directory where the CUDA toolkit is installed.  The
build computer itself need not be CUDA-capable or have drivers supporting
CUDA or the particular CUDA version being built for.  So far, CUDA 2.1 - 2.3,
3.1 - 3.3, 4.0 - 4.1, 6.0, 6.5, and 8.0, and 10.1 have been used on various
systems.  A build for one CUDA version can always use CUDA on a system whose
drivers support a higher version.

The setup scripts automatically configure to use OpenMP in cases where they
think it is available.

You need an Oracle or OpenJDK version of the Java Development Kit to build
Etomo. Version 1.7 or higher is required and there should be no problems using
the latest version.  On a 64-bit system, it does not matter if this is 32 or
64-bit. A suitable JDK should be available in most versions of Linux; and on
Windows or Mac you should install a package from OpenJDK to avoid license
issues.  You must place the bin of the JDK on your path on Windows, and on
Linux if you use one not that is not a native install.

IMOD is built with version 4 of libtiff to support large files,
specifically tiff-4.0.1.  Source for this or earlier versions is obtained
from links at http://simplesystems.org/libtiff/.  However, IMOD still builds with
version 3 of libtiff if that is what is on your system.  In this case, you
need to have a libtiff-devel package installed, as well as libtiff.

IMOD can be built to use floating point FFT libraries from FFTW instead its
own FFT routines by setting the environment variable FFTW3_DIR.  On Mac, or on
Linux with custom-built libraries, this should point to an FFTW install
directory containing lib and include subdirectories; on Linux with libraries
from an install package, it should point to the system library directory.  On
Windows, it should point to a directory that contains the .lib, .dll, and .h
files (see below).  When using Intel compilers, IMOD can be build to use the
Math Kernel Library (MKL) FFT routines by giving the setup option -mkl or
setting the environment variable IMOD_SETUP_USE_MKL; both of these override a
setting of FFTW3_DIR.

As of 4.8.2, IMOD can be built with HDF5 by setting the environment variable
HDF5_DIR.  On Mac, or on Linux with custom-built libraries, this should point
to an FFTW install directory containing lib and include subdirectories; on
Linux with libraries from an install package, it should point to the system
library directory.  On Windows, a directory with bin and include
subdirectories is expected (see below).  Version 1.8.12 is used in the
distributions.

setup has a number of options that can be listed with "setup -h".  Currently
the main body of the setup code is in setup2 because that needed to be
converted to Unix line endings in Cygwin.  To do most of the system-specific
work, setup sources an appropriate file in the machines directory: rhlinux,
macosx, or cygwin.

The install commands will build .1 and .html versions of all man pages, 
install the .1 files in man of the install directory, collect the .html files
and other help files under html in the source directory, build IMOD.qch
from all those help files and install it in html of the install directory.  If
the development environment is not on the various appropriate PATHs,
installqtlib must be run to make the installation generally usable.
If the install directory is omitted, then installation will be into bin, lib,
man, and html directories under the top-level source directory.  This is fine
for personal use, although on Windows installqtlib must also be run.


BUILDING ON LINUX

IMOD should build readily on recent Linux versions provided that gfortran and
a Qt 4 or 5 development package are installed.

To build Qt 5.9.3 under RHEL7, Qt was unpacked in a local directory, an install
directory /usr/local/Qt/Qt-5.9.3-RHEL7 was created, QMAKESPEC was undefined,
and Qt was built using gcc/g++ with the commands:

configure -prefix /usr/local/Qt/Qt-5.9.3-RHEL7 -no-rpath \
-nomake examples -nomake tests -opensource -confirm-license -qt-xcb \
-skip webchannel -skip webengine -skip websockets -skip qtwebview -sql-mysql \
-sql-sqlite
make -j12      (parallel make works fine)
make install

The package mysql++-devel.x86_64 was installed to enable the option
-plugin-sql-mysql.

Additional dependencies were necessary to build Qt 5.12, which is being used
instead of 5.9 (available as a system install) because widgets look somehwat
better on a high-DPI monitor.  These were installed with:

sudo yum install xcb-util xcb-util-devel 'xcb-util-*-devel' libxkbcommon-devel

Various arrangements for Qt development tools installed from packages should
be handled correctly in Fedora, RHEL, and Ubuntu.  setup will detect the lack
of a QTDIR/include and set its Qt include variable to /usr/include (the
location on earlier Fedora) if /usr/include/Qt exists, otherwise to
/usr/include/qt4 or /usr/include/qt4 (location on Ubuntu).  The arrangement on
RHEL6 and 7 and later Fedoras is also handled correctly, where QTDIR should be
defined as /usr/lib64/qt4 or /usr/lib64/qt5 on a 64-bit system.

To compile IMOD, QTDIR must be defined, $QTDIR/bin must be on the path, and
$QTDIR/lib must be on the LD_LIBRARY_PATH if it exists (if it does not exist,
e.g., on RHEL6, there should not be a problem).

The compilers can be either gcc and gfortran, or the Intel C++ and Fortran
compilers (versions 11 to 15 can be used; a version higher than 11 on RHEL7).
If the Intel compilers are on the path, they will be used by default; this can
be overriden with the "-c gnu" or "-c gfortran" setup option.  Either way will
support OpenMP and CUDA for "current" compilers (e.g., gcc 4.4.7 in RHEL6 or
4.8.5 in RHEL7).  If Intel compilers are used, QMAKESPEC must be set to
linux-icc.

As mentioned above, development packages for TIFF, FFTW, and HDF can be
installed and compiled against.  Here are instructions for building these
packages instead:

To build version 4 TIFF libraries, unpack the libtiff distribution and decide
on a directory to install to.  Build with:
./configure --prefix=install_directory
make [-jn]        (parallel make works fine)
make install

Then define an environment variable TIFF4_DIR to point to the install
directory.

To build version 3 of FFTW, unpack the source code and decide on a directory
to install to.  Build with:
./configure --enable-openmp --enable-float --disable-fortran -enable-shared \
--prefix=install_directory
make [-jn]        (parallel make works fine)
make install

The libraries in IMOD are built with the Intel compiler by setting the
environment variable CC to "icc".  Define an environment variable FFTW3_DIR to point to
the install directory.

To build HDF5, unpack the source code and decide on a directory to install to,
or just use the default of installing to a directory hdf5 in the top of the
source tree.
./configure [--prefix=install_directory]
make [-jn]
make install

Then define an environment variable HDF5_DIR to point to the install
directory.


To build and install IMOD for general use:

1. "./setup -i [install directory]" to set the install directory.

2. "make" to make all the programs.

3. "make install"

4. "installqtlib" to copy install files to the install directory, as well as
   Qt libraries and other libraries needed for a distribution.

The last step can be skipped for a personal installation in the source
directory, as long as all libraries used are on your LD_LIBRARY_PATH.


Static builds in Linux for minimal dependencies

The setup scripts and Makefiles allow programs to be built with minimal
dependencies except for Qt-based programs, which will still depend on
shared Qt libraries.  This makes individual programs available for easy
inclusion in other packages.  Another limitation is that if you want to
use MKL-based FFTs, you can only build IMOD libraries dynamically.  However, a
static build can be done with FFTW.

The setup options controlling the creation and use of static libraries are:

-no_shared:
  Make and link static IMOD libraries.  This is the option that is
  incompatible with MKL-based FFTs

-static #:
  Link to static Intel libraries.  # can be 1 to leave a dependency on
  libiomp5.so, or 2 to link to the static OpenMP library too, which is
  strongly recommended against in the icc man page but seems to work OK.

-other[_static] #:
  Link to other static libraries: # can be the sum of 1 for CUDA, 2 for TIFF,
  4 for HDF5, and 8 for FFTW3 libraries.
 
For the last option, libraries can be either custom-built or in the system
from install packages.  On RHEL7, fftw-static, libtiff-static, and hdf5-static
are available.  When using a system static libtiff, be sure to define
TIFF4_DIR to the system library directory (/usr/lib64 on Linux).  Linking
statically to HDF5 or TIFF will still leave a dependency on lz.so and libjpeg.so;
the latter is prone to run into version incompatibilities between RHEL and
Ubuntu.  On RHEL7, zlib-static and libjpeg-turbo-static are available to
eliminate these dependencies.  Finally, linking to a system-installed HDF
library will leave a dependency on libsz.so and libaec.so from the libaec
package, which is not in the default RHEL7 install.

Statically linking to CUDA will give very large executables for the CUDA-based
programs.


BUILDING ON MAC OS X

These notes describe how to achieve a complete build of IMOD.  However, this
is not necessary for building plugins or developing C programs; a minimal
setup is described at the end of this section.

Generally, you should have the latest version of Xcode tools for your system
installed; but see the notes below about using an older version in order to
install an integrated gfortran.


Qt for Mac OSX

Here are two recent experiences, first building Qt 5.10.1 under OS
10.12 with compatibility with 10.11.  Xcode 8.3.2 was installed, then XCode
7.3.1 was downloaded, the package opened up, and the SDK was copied into the
XCode installation in /Applications by right clicking and selecting
"Show package Contents" -> Contents -> Developer -> Platforms ->
MacOSX.platform -> Developer -> SDKS -> MaxOSX10.11.sdk
The corresponding location can be opened in /Applications/XCode.app and
MaxOSX10.11.sdk dragged or copied and pasted into the SDKS folder.

Qt 5.10.1 was unpacked into a local directory ~/qt-src-5.10.1 and a directory
/usr/local/Qt/Qt-5.10.1 was created.
unset QTDIR
unset QMAKESPEC
export MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=10.11

./configure -release -prefix /usr/local/Qt/Qt-5.10.1 -c++std c++11 -no-rpath \
-nomake examples -opensource -confirm-license -nomake tests -sdk macosx10.11 \
-skip webchannel -skip webengine -skip websockets -skip qtwebview
make -j4
make install
cd /usr/local/Qt/Qt-5.10.1/
mv include/ badInclude
ln -s ~/qt-src-5.10.1/qtbase/include .

That last step is needed because the Qt developers decided to include headers
in the installed library frameworks instead of in a proper include
directory.  The IMOD dist procedure removes headers from the frameworks when
packing them in a distribution.

Qt 5.13.1 and 5.14.2 were built on OS 10.14 with XCode 10.3, using essentially
the same commands, but with

./configure -release -prefix /usr/local/Qt/Qt-5.13.1  -no-rpath -c++std c++14 \
-nomake examples -opensource -confirm-license -nomake tests -skip qtwebchannel \
-skip qtwebengine -skip qtwebsockets -skip qtwebview -skip qtandroidextras \
-skip qtwayland -skip qtwebglplugin -skip qtwinextras -skip qtx11extras \
-skip qtcanvas3d -skip qt3d -skip qtmultimedia -skip qtpurchasing \
-skip qtquickcontrols -skip qtquickcontrols2

Perhaps more modules could be skipped, but these will save an enormous and lengthy
build of webengine. "make clean" can be done.  If you did not skip qtwebengine, deleting
qtwebengine/src/core/Release will save ~25 GB.

To build IMOD, QTDIR must be defined, $QTDIR/bin must be on the path, and
$QTDIR/lib must be on the DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH.


TIFF and other libraries for Mac OSX

You should build and install TIFF libraries from source; installing JPEG is
optional to allow JPEG compression to be used in TIFF files (libz is on the
Mac already).  We use jpeg-8b from http://www.ijg.org/.
We use static libraries which are linked into libiimod.dylib.  When we
tried to use dynamic libraries, 3dmod and etomo would not run because a system
library tried to call back into the libtiff and libjpeg that we had placed on 
the DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH.

On early 64-bit Macs it was necessary to set flags to obtain a 64-bit build,
but this is no longer necessary.  This was done by defining environment
variables both CFLAGS and CXXFLAGS to be '-g -O2 -m64'

For jpeg:
./configure
make 
sudo make install

For TIFF,
./configure --with-jpeg-include-dir=/usr/local/include \
--with-jpeg-lib-dir=/usr/local/lib -without-x
make
sudo make install

However, on OS 10.12, both /usr/local/libtiff.dylib and
/usr/local/libjpeg.dylib needed to be renamed or removed in order for Qt not
to try to use those incorrectly.  (The .a files are used in the IMOD build).

To build version 3 of FFTW and HDF5, follow the instructions above for Linux.


Compilers for Mac OSX

You need to have a Fortran compiler.  Two options are the Intel compiler
(version 11.1 on OS X 10.6, 2013 on 10.8, 2017 on 10.12) and a version of
gfortran. Our earlier experience with gfortran was with "integrated" versions
from http://r.research.att.com/tools/ that allowed OpenMP to be used.  This
experience is not relevant for current OS's, other than
that gfortran from http://hpc.sourceforge.net/ should be avoided.

On OS X 10.8, we obtained gfortran 4.8 for OS X 10.8 from
http://gcc.gnu.org/wiki/GFortranBinaries. This appears to be the best source
of gfortran for current OS's.  The setup script assumes that there is no
OpenMP when building with gfortran under 10.8.  This may no longer be
appropriate with the latest gfortran from that source.

The setup scripts provide for three options for compilers on Intel Macs:
Native gcc     gfortran       (OpenMP works if gcc 4.2 and gfortran integrated)
Native gcc     Intel Fortran  (OpenMP works if gcc 4.2 and gfortran integrated)
Intel C++      Intel Fortran  (OpenMP works)

The setup script will try to do the right thing if you have the
true location of one or both Intel compilers on your path (i.e., the presence
of links in /usr/bin is ignored).  Namely, if the true location of the Intel
C++ compiler is on the path, it will assume Intel Fortran is to be used
(option 3); if only the true location of the Intel Fortran compiler is on the
path, it will use that and gcc (option 2); if neither is on the path it will
use gnu compilers.  These defaults can be overriden by "-c intel", which
forces it to try to use both Intel C++ and Fortran compilers; "-c ifort" which
forces it to use Intel Fortran but use Intel C++ only if its true location is
on the path; or "-c gnu" or "-c gfortran", which forces it to gnu compilers.

On OS 10.12, CUDA 8.0 could not be used with the clang version 8 compiler.
The NVIDIA instructions to handle this were useless, but instead the command
line tools for XCode 7.3.1 were installed, but not selected with xcode-select.
This environment variable is then set to use them in the CUDA compilations:
export NVCC_TOOL_ENV="env DEVELOPER_DIR=/Library/Developer/CommandLineTools/".
It appears that XCode updates remove these tools, so they have to be
reinstalled when that happens.

Also, CUDA 8 appears to install in /usr/local/cuda, but this directory
contains only links to corresponding directories in
/Developer/NVIDIA/CUDA-8.0.  In order for "make dist" to pack the CUDA
libraries, it was necessary to set CUDA_DIR to this location.

On OS 10.12, additional variables need to be set to work around System
Integrity Protection and to build for an earlier version of OSX:
export SIP_SCRIPT_LIBPATH="$DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH"
export SIP_SCRIPT_FRAMEPATH="$DYLD_FRAMEWORK_PATH"
export SIP_COMMAND_PATHENV="DYLD_FRAMEWORK_PATH=$SIP_SCRIPT_FRAMEPATH \
  DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH=$SIP_SCRIPT_LIBPATH"
export MACOSX_VERSION_MIN=10.9

The latter makes the rest of the IMOD build mimic what qmake put into
Makefiles for the programs using Qt.


The procedure is now:

1. "./setup -i [install directory]" to set the install directory.

2. "make" to make all the programs.

3. "make install"

4. "./installqtlib" to copy Qt library and install files to install directory


A Minimal Setup for Mac OS X

You can get through the IMOD setup without a Fortran compiler and without TIFF
libraries.  You will still need Qt.  See the instructions above for using the
Qt 5.4 binary package. 

To build plugins, you also need a compiled copy of 3dmod in the 3dmod
directory.  You can get this from an appropriate recent binary distribution of
IMOD, for example:
cp -r /Applications/IMOD/bin/3dmod.app 3dmod/
./setup -c gnu -no_tiff
make clibs
At this point, you should be able to cd into any directory except flib and
3dmod and do a successful make.


BUILDING UNDER CYGWIN/WINDOWS WITH INTEL COMPILERS

The build under Windows requires Microsoft Visual Studio, 2008 or 2013 preferred.

This build also requires Intel Fortran and C++ compilers.
 
The PATH, LIB, and INCLUDE environment variables must be set with the entries
that they acquire when running the startup scripts for Microsoft Visual Studio
(obtained by running the menu entry to get a command prompt) and the two Intel
compilers (obtained with the two menu entries for getting the compiler
environment).

On some machines, the Intel compilers require a setting for the TMP or TEMP
variable to a location that you can write in, such as C:\cygwin\usr\tmp


Qt for Windows

Qt 4.7.4 was built by unzipping a .zip package in C:/Qt and renaming it to
4.7.4.  Untarring a tar package with cygwin tar is also possible but
configure.exe gets overwritten by the configure script, so it has to be copied
from elsewhere or maybe explicitly extracted by itself.  QMAKESPEC was defined
as win32-msvc2008 and C:/Qt/4.7.4/bin was put on the PATH.  It was not
necessary to take /bin/sh.exe off the path or install a perl separate from the
one in Cygwin.  A command prompt was opened in VS2008 (x64 for 64-bit) and
these commands used:

./configure -release -fast -no-exceptions -qt-zlib -qt-gif -qt-libpng \
-qt-libmng -no-libtiff -qt-libjpeg -no-phonon -no-phonon-backend -no-webkit \
-nomake demos -nomake examples -no-qt3support
nmake
nmake clean

Problems with OpenGL were encountered when using the binary install packages
for Qt 5.5, so Qt 5.9.3 was built in VS2013.  Following advice
online, Python 2.7 and Perl (from ActiveState) were installed, although it is
possible that the Perl and Python in Cygwin would have been adequate.  To
enable a parallel make, jom was obtained from links at https://wiki.qt.io/Jom
and unpacked into C:/Qt/jom.  A Qt 5.9.3 tar.xz source package was unpacked in
C:\Qt and renamed to 5.9.3.

The file configure.bat in the qtbase subdirectory must be edited to remove
icl.exe from this line:
for %%C in (cl.exe icl.exe g++.exe perl.exe jom.exe) do set %%C=%%~$PATH:C

Open a Visual Studio 2013 command prompt.
cd C:\Qt\5.9.3
set PYTHON=C:\Program Files\Python2.7    (or wherever you installed it)
set QTDIR=C:\Qt\5.9.3\qtbase
set PERL=C:\Perl64
set PATH=C:\Qt\jom;%QTDIR%\bin;%QTDIR%\gnuwin32\bin;%PYTHON%;%PYTHON%\DLLs;%PERL%\site\bin;%PERL%\bin;%PATH%
configure -release -opensource -platform win32-msvc2013 -opengl desktop -nomake examples -nomake tests -skip webchannel -skip webengine -skip websockets -skip qtwebview
jom -j6   (or whatever number of cores you want to use)

Finally, Qt 5.12.4 was built with a better procedure that installed in a
separate directory from the source.  The same Perl from ActiveState was used
but a standard Windows distribution of Python was used instead.  A Qt 5.1.4
tar.xz source package was unpacked in C:\Qt.  It is no longer necessary to
modify configure.bat.

Open a Visual Studio 2015 x64 command prompt
cd C:\Qt\qt-everywhere-src-5.12.4
set QTDIR=C:\Qt\qt-everywhere-src-5.12.4\qtbase
set PYTHON=C:\Python36                  (or wherever you installed it)
set PERL=C:\Perl64
set PATH=C:\Qt\jom;%QTDIR%\bin;%QTDIR%\gnuwin32\bin;%PYTHON%;%PYTHON%\DLLs;%PERL%\site\bin;%PERL%\bin;%PATH%
configure -release -prefix C:/Qt/5.12.4 -opensource -confirm-license -platform win32-msvc2015 -opengl desktop -nomake examples -nomake tests -skip webchannel -skip webengine -skip websockets -skip qtwebview -skip qtandroidextras -skip qtwayland -skip qtwebglplugin -skip qtwinextras -skip qtx11extras -skip qtcanvas3d
jom -j6   (or whatever number of cores you want to use)
jom install


TIFF and Other Libraries for Windows

TIFF include files and an import library are contained in the IMOD package (in
sysdep/win) and the software will build without the TIFF library present, but
installqtlib will not run.  For 32-bit, you can just install TIFF libraries
from GnuWin32: http://gnuwin32.sourceforge.net/packages/tiff.htm. You want the
ordinary version rather than the one using the Win32 API.  You can get the
TIFF binaries in the "Complete packages, except sources".  Version 3.5.7 is
the source of the import library packaged in the IMOD source.

IMOD is built with libtiff from http://simplesystems.org/libtiff/, jpeg-8b
from http://www.ijg.org/, and zlib-1.2.5 from http://www.zlib.net/.  These
packages should all be unpacked in a common directory without spaces in its
path.  For each package, either rename the package directory or create a link
so that the common directory contains subdirectories named "tiff", "jpeg", and
"zlib".  Define an environment variable TIFF_JPEG_ZLIB with the location of
this directory, and IMOD setup will be able to find all of the needed files
from the packages.

ZLIB:
In win32/Makefile.msc:
1) Add inffast.obj to the OBJ line (unless it is there in the version you
have)
2) Modify SHAREDLIB to be imodzlib1.dll
(This is following the documentation's strong suggestion that a 
DLL linked against a  MSVCRT from Visual Studio instead of MSVCRT.DLL be given
a different name)
nmake /f win32/Makefile.msc

JPEG:
cp jconfig.vc jconfig.h
cp makefile.vc Makefile

modify jmorecfg.h to have:
#define GLOBAL(type)          __declspec(dllexport) type
#define EXTERN(type)          extern __declspec(dllexport) type

modify Makefile:
1) Add /D_CRT_SECURE_NO_WARNINGS to CFLAGS
2) Add libjpeg.dll to the "all:" line and
3) Add
libjpeg.dll: $(LIBOBJECTS)
        $(RM) libjpeg.dll libjpeg_i.lib
        link /DLL /OUT:libjpeg.dll /IMPLIB:libjpeg_i.lib $(LIBOBJECTS)

nmake /f Makefile

The nmake command also required "nodebug=1" for 32-bit Windows but not 64-bit.
When building with VS2013, it was necessary to copy win32.mak from elsewhere
into the jpeg directory (on our machine, a suitable version was found in 
C:\Program Files (x86)\Microsoft SDKs\Windows\v7.1A\Include\).

TIFF:
modify nmake.opt:
#
JPEG_SUPPORT    = 1
JPEGDIR         = "<Full path to common directory>/jpeg-8b"
JPEG_INCLUDE    = -I$(JPEGDIR)
JPEG_LIB        = $(JPEGDIR)/libjpeg_i.lib

#
ZIP_SUPPORT     = 1
ZLIBDIR         = "<Full path to common directory>/zlib-1.2.5"
ZLIB_INCLUDE    = -I$(ZLIBDIR)
ZLIB_LIB        = $(ZLIBDIR)/zdll.lib

and add /D_CRT_NONSTDC_NO_DEPRECATE to OPTFLAGS

nmake /f Makefile.vc

Open a Visual Studio Command Prompt through Visual Studio Tools for the nmake
commands.

To build IMOD with version 3 of FFTW, simply unzip the appropriate DLL package
from http://www.fftw.org/install/windows.html into a directory.  Follow the
instructions there to create the .lib file:
  lib /def:libfftw3f-3.def                (on 32-bit)
  lib /machine:x64 /def:libfftw3f-3.def   (on 64-bit)
Then point the environment variable FFTW3_DIR to this directory.

To build HDF5, cmake is needed; a binary distribution for Windows works.
In your zlib directory, first copy zdll.lib to imodzlib1.obj.
Create your own HDF install directory somewhere and set the environment variable
HDF5 to point to it.

Unpack the HDF source into a directory.  Edit the file
config/cmake/cacheinit.cmake to change ON to OFF in these lines.
SET (HDF5_BUILD_CPP_LIB ON CACHE BOOL "Build HDF5 C++ Library" FORCE)
SET (HDF5_BUILD_EXAMPLES ON CACHE BOOL "Build HDF5 Library Examples" FORCE)
SET (HDF5_BUILD_FORTRAN ON CACHE BOOL "Build FORTRAN support" FORCE)
SET (HDF5_ENABLE_SZIP_SUPPORT ON CACHE BOOL "Use SZip Filter" FORCE)
